# FUMOLA: THE BIG PICTURE #
# Fumola is an experimental language whose execution model is Adapton: a    #
# program's run leaves behind a graph of what it named, read and demanded,  #
# and that graph is what the next run reuses.                               #
#                                                                           #
# Its surface is deliberately ordinary -- functions, records, variants,     #
# modules, pattern matching, arithmetic, taken from Motoko -- so that the   #
# way a program runs is the part worth looking at.                          #
#                                                                           #
# The four livelits, in brief:                                              #
#                                                                           #
#   ^fumola_new(id, mode)             declare a runtime                     #
#   ^fumola_put_force(id, name, src)  run src in a named thunk              #
#   ^fumola_eval(id, src)             run src on a force-free stack         #
#   ^fumola_with(id, name, src, v)    the same, with v bound as input       #
#                                                                           #
# The slides after this one take an idea each. This one is the map.         #

# ADAPTON, BRIEFLY #
# A run builds a demanded computation graph: cells, which hold values, and  #
# thunks, which are suspended computations, with an edge for every action a #
# thunk took -- every cell written, every cell read, every thunk forced --  #
# kept in the order it took them.                                           #
#                                                                           #
# The graph exists so that a later run can be repaired rather than redone.  #
# When a cell changes, the thunks that read it are the ones whose results   #
# are in question; the rest of the graph is still good, and is reused as it #
# stands. What makes that addressable is that every cell and thunk has a    #
# name: the run after an edit has to recognise the node it is about to      #
# recompute as the same node as last time, and a name is how it does.       #

# THE PRIMITIVES #
# There are not many. Two ways to make a node, two ways to depend on one,   #
# one way to look without depending, and a way to say where new nodes live. #
#                                                                           #
#   thunk { e }         suspend a computation; a thunk is an ordinary value #
#   `n := e             put: write e into the cell `n, giving a pointer     #
#   get(`n)   or  @p    get: read a cell, recording a dependency on it      #
#   force(p)            demand a thunk, recording a dependency on it        #
#   peek(`n)            read without recording anything; answers an option  #
#   do within space `s { e }    where the nodes made inside e belong        #
#                                                                           #
# Not a primitive, but the idiom the rest are usually reached through:      #
#                                                                           #
#   force(`n := thunk { e })                                                #
#                                                                           #
# Name a thunk, put a computation in it, demand it. Running it again with   #
# the same name is what an edit is -- and it is what the livelit below does #
# to itself every time you change the program inside it.                    #
let answer : Int =
  ^fumola_put_force(0, "`gcd", "Gcd.gcd(12, 18)") in

# The other livelit runs with no thunk around it, so the put and the get    #
# here are recorded against the graph's root rather than a computation.     #
let counted : Int =
  ^fumola_eval(0, "`count := 41; get(`count) + 1") in

# NAMES ARE VALUES #
# A name is a symbol: an identifier written with a leading backtick, or a   #
# number, and symbols compose by application as deeply as you like. So a    #
# name can be computed -- which is what lets a recursive algorithm give     #
# each of its nodes a name derived from its position rather than from a     #
# counter.                                                                  #
let aName : String =
  ^fumola_put_force(0, "`aName", "`adapton(`settings)") in

# A name has two dimensions rather than one. Space is where a cell lives,   #
# so a subcomputation can allocate without colliding with its caller; time  #
# is when it holds what it holds, which is what lets one name carry a       #
# history rather than a single value.                                       #

# TWO MODES #
# Which mode a program is in is decided by whether a force is underway.     #
#                                                                           #
# The archivist runs inside one, and the tracked operations -- put, get,    #
# and force itself -- belong to it, because there is a computation in       #
# progress for a dependency to be recorded against. The editor runs on a    #
# force-free stack: it is where changes come from, and where resetting the  #
# store and reading the graph belong. peek is the editor's, but stays       #
# available inside a force on purpose, since reading a cell without         #
# recording a dependency is exactly how you look at a computation while it  #
# runs.                                                                     #
#                                                                           #
# That distinction is the one between the two livelits above.               #

# TWO SEMANTICS #
# The primitives have two implementations, and a runtime runs one or the    #
# other. Graphical keeps the graph proper -- nodes, edges, each thunk's     #
# trace in the order it ran, and an event history -- which is what makes    #
# the graph readable from inside the language, and what repair will need.   #
# Simple keeps a store and no more: cells, and thunks with their cached     #
# results. It is cheap and predictable, and it keeps no graph, so there is  #
# nothing to introspect and nothing to repair.                              #
#                                                                           #
# Slide 6 declares a graphical runtime and reads its event log as a table.  #

(answer, counted, aName)
